    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Tossing coins"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Toss a coin and a die together. Which outcomes are possible, and what is the chance of a head with an even number? Probability counts favourable outcomes against all outcomes."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "sample space",
          "P(A) = n(A)/n(S)",
          "0 to 1",
          "complement",
          "addition theorem",
          "mutually exclusive",
          "die"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "The sample space S is the set of all possible outcomes of an experiment, and an event is a subset of S.\nProbability of event A = n(A)/n(S), where all outcomes are equally likely.\nThe probability of any event lies between 0 and 1; a sure event has P = 1 and an impossible event P = 0.\nThe complement of A, written A', has P(A') = 1 - P(A).\nAddition theorem: for any two events A and B, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B).\nIf A and B are mutually exclusive (cannot occur together), P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).\nThrowing a die: S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, so P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2."
        }
      },
